## Review Findings

### Warning — SCSS media query ordering splits selector definition across the file

**Location:** `/workspace/repo/frontend/src/app/learn/fretboard-flash/fretboard-flash-page/fretboard-flash-page.scss`, lines 66–79

The `@media (max-width: 600px)` block redefines `.fretboard-flash-page` as a separate rule at the bottom of the file, splitting its definition from the base styles at line 5. The conventional SCSS pattern is to nest the media query inside the selector so all rules for that selector stay together:

```scss
.fretboard-flash-page {
  // base styles ...
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    padding: 1rem 0.5rem;
    gap: 1rem;
  }
}
```

This applies equally to `.note-area` and `.note-display` overrides in the same media block.

---

### Suggestion — `--gt-accent` color does not adapt between light and dark themes

**Location:** `/workspace/repo/frontend/src/app/learn/fretboard-flash/fretboard-flash-page/fretboard-flash-page.scss`, line 26

`--gt-accent` resolves to `$accent-orange` (#fd8d32) in both `[tuiTheme='light']` and `[tuiTheme='dark']` in `/workspace/repo/frontend/src/app/theme/_theme.scss`. The note letter will always be orange regardless of theme. Contrast on dark blue is acceptable, but if theme-aware text color was intended, `--gt-text` or `--tui-text-action` would adapt correctly. Worth a deliberate confirmation before closing the task.

---

### Suggestion — DOM order layout tests are brittle and duplicate existing coverage

**Location:** `/workspace/repo/frontend/src/app/learn/fretboard-flash/fretboard-flash-page/fretboard-flash-page.spec.ts`, lines 158–181

The two `compareDocumentPosition` tests assert structural template ordering. They will break on any legitimate template restructure unrelated to functional behavior. The existing state-based tests already confirm the correct elements render in the correct states. DOM order assertions are better suited to accessibility/focus order audits. Consider removing these two tests unless tab/reading order is a stated accessibility requirement.

---

No Critical findings. The `note-area` wrapper to prevent layout shift is a sound pattern, the component stays thin, and state is correctly read from signals.

